Z||000003||theogre||09-17-1999||10:47 PM||||hill,,<P>those the replacement bushings for the aluminum bone? what motor? <P>I was thinking about using the poly in the AL bone, but mine is not straight to the block. Is anyone elses 4cyl bone straight? I was considering shifting the motor on the lower mounts, but it's nearly an inch off. The CV joints on the other hand are near centered perfectly...... Shifting a CV an inch is a really bad thing.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||09-18-1999||12:40 AM||||Ogre, I'm not sure exactly what you mean. I looked at the dogbone on my 2.5 just now and it looks perpendicular to the engine. The dogbone itself is straight. I don't recall for sure, but I think you have to replace the whole dogbone on a 2.5, however, with the 2.8 you have the option of installing new bushings. Somebody correct me if I'm wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-18-1999||12:29 PM||||yeah, that's what I ment... My motor has the bone off about an inch to the right..... the brackets don't line up. Was wondering how many others had the same problem.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||09-19-1999||12:52 AM||||I have an 86 SE 2.8 V6 and you need to be careful because I just found out that there are a couple of stock dogbones for the 2.8, <BR>one of which has tapered bushing (two cones with their points connected) and won't accept the straight cylindrical replacement poly bushings.<P>I don't know how to tell the difference, but save yourself some trouble and just buy the Ultimate Dogbone if you want poly on your strut.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||09-19-1999||09:59 AM||||Ogre, I looked at my 2.5 again and the dogbone brackets that mount to the engine have some weird off-set and angles to them. Maybe yours is bent or tweeked. Maybe you can round up another set of mounting brackets and get it to position correctly again. I do know that those factory 2.5 dogbones wear out way too fast.||||reg||1
